  # ------------------------------------------------------------- the stops
  stops:

  - group: GIBBS
    call: Talk to Commander Abiola, at the sample tray in Plant Control
    task: Sort four labelled samples into atoms, molecules and ions.
    title: Labels on the sample tray
    at: sample-tray
    person: abiola
    reason: The ledger cannot be trusted until every sample type is identified.
    concept: Atoms, molecules and ions, and the mole as a way of counting them
    place: Plant Control
    # Bible: "Question card story setup - exact player copy"
    # KEYSTONE (authoring metadata, master brief §3.3/§3.4):
    #   Structure sets behaviour · INTRODUCE · L1 · obstacle
    scene: >-
      Before the plant can compare its records, classify the four sample symbols so the ledger
      counts atoms, molecules, and ions correctly. This gives every later calculation a
      trustworthy starting point.
    # Bible: "Question card story-science connection - exact player copy"
    motivation: >-
      Correct labels prevent the plant from counting unlike particles as though they were
      interchangeable.
    # Bible: "Key concepts, explained here", the mission's own teaching block.
    background:
    - >-
      Matter is described at several levels. An atom is one neutral element unit; a molecule is
      bonded atoms with no net charge; an ion has a net charge. Chemists convert a measured mass to
      moles using molar mass, then convert moles to particles with Avogadro's number, 6.022 x 10^23
      mol^-1. A balanced equation is an atom ledger: coefficients may change the number of
      molecules, but atoms are not created or destroyed.
    assumes:
    - a chemical formula says which elements are present and in what ratio
    format: CHOICE
    # Bible: "Question card prompt - exact player copy"
    question: Which classification is completely correct?
    choices:
    - Ar is an atom; CH4 and CO2 are molecules; H+ is an ion.
    - All four are molecules because each symbol describes matter.
    - Ar and H+ are atoms; CH4 and CO2 are ions.
    - CO2 is an atom because it is one chemical formula.
    answer: Ar is an atom; CH4 and CO2 are molecules; H+ is an ion.
    answerText: >-
      Ar is an atom; CH4 and CO2 are molecules; H+ is an ion.
    why: >-
      Ar is a single neutral atom. CH4 and CO2 contain bonded atoms and are neutral molecules.
      H+ has a positive charge, so it is an ion. "Particle" is the umbrella word; it does not
      erase these distinctions.
    rebuttals:
    - A formula can name an atom, molecule, or ion; the word formula does not decide.
    - H+ is charged, while CH4 and CO2 have no net charge.
    - One written formula can contain several atoms.

  - group: KINET
    call: Go to the conversion board, in Plant Control
    task: Estimate how many methane molecules the shortfall stands for.
    title: How much methane is missing
    at: conv-board
    reason: The tank scale and reactor model must use the same amount units.
    concept: Atoms, molecules and ions, and the mole as a way of counting them
    place: Plant Control
    # KEYSTONE: Follow the amount · INTRODUCE · L2 · obstacle
    scene: >-
      With the samples labeled correctly, convert the reported methane shortage from tank mass
      into the molecule count used by the reactor model. The result lets the two records be
      compared without guessing.
    motivation: >-
      The crew cannot compare a tank scale with molecular production until both are expressed
      through moles.
    background:
    - >-
      Matter is described at several levels. An atom is one neutral element unit; a molecule is
      bonded atoms with no net charge; an ion has a net charge. Chemists convert a measured mass to
      moles using molar mass, then convert moles to particles with Avogadro's number, 6.022 x 10^23
      mol^-1. A balanced equation is an atom ledger: coefficients may change the number of
      molecules, but atoms are not created or destroyed.
    assumes:
    - a mole is a fixed number of particles, whatever the substance
    format: BALLPARK
    question: Estimate the number of methane molecules represented by the shortfall.
    estimate:
      prompt: The wall shows a 1.60 x 10^3 kg methane shortfall, and the reactor model wants that as molecules.
      question: Estimate the number of methane molecules represented by the shortfall.
      relationship: molecules = mass in grams / molar mass x Avogadro's number
      labels:
      - 1.60×10³ kg  (methane short of schedule)
      - 1000 g/kg  (kilograms to grams)
      - 16.04 g/mol  (molar mass of methane)
      - 6.022×10²³ molecules/mol  (Avogadro's number)
      - 44.01 g/mol  (molar mass of carbon dioxide)
      values: [1600, 1000, 16.04, 6.022e23, 44.01]
      slots: 4
      template: '{0} × {1} ÷ {2} × {3}'
      formula: a*b/c*d
      correct: [0, 1, 2, 3]
      target: 6.01e28
      tolerance: 3.6e27
      units: CH4 molecules
      solution: 1.60 × 10⁶ g ÷ 16.04 g/mol ≈ 9.98 × 10⁴ mol, and × 6.022 × 10²³ ≈ 6.01 × 10²⁸ molecules.
      explanation: Kilograms must become grams before dividing by grams per mole, and the moles that come out are then multiplied up into particles.
    answerText: >-
      The shortfall represents about 6.01 x 10^28 methane molecules.
    why: >-
      Kilograms must become grams before dividing by grams per mole. The result is about 9.98 x
      10^4 mol, and each mole represents Avogadro's number of molecules. Multiplying mass
      directly by Avogadro's number skips molar mass and gives meaningless units.

  - group: SOIL
    call: Go to the technician's spreadsheet, in Plant Control
    task: Put the five steps of the mass-to-molecules conversion in order.
    title: Rebuild the conversion
    at: unit-board
    reason: A deleted conversion could create the shortage the crew thinks it sees.
    concept: Atoms, molecules and ions, and the mole as a way of counting them
    place: Plant Control
    # KEYSTONE: Follow the amount · PRACTICE · L2 · clue
    scene: >-
      Because a deleted formula could create a false shortage, rebuild the full
      mass-to-molecules conversion before trusting yesterday's estimate. A complete unit path
      will expose whether the estimate is artificial before repairs begin.
    motivation: >-
      A broken unit chain could create a false shortfall and send technicians after a leak that
      does not exist.
    background:
    - >-
      Matter is described at several levels. An atom is one neutral element unit; a molecule is
      bonded atoms with no net charge; an ion has a net charge. Chemists convert a measured mass to
      moles using molar mass, then convert moles to particles with Avogadro's number, 6.022 x 10^23
      mol^-1. A balanced equation is an atom ledger: coefficients may change the number of
      molecules, but atoms are not created or destroyed.
    assumes:
    - a unit can be cancelled only against the same unit
    format: SEQUENCE
    question: Put the methane conversion workflow in order.
    cards:
    - Read tank mass in kg
    - Convert kg to g
    - Divide by CH4 molar mass
    - Multiply by Avogadro's number
    - Report molecules with units
    order: [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]
    answerText: >-
      Use mass, convert to grams, divide by molar mass, multiply by Avogadro's number, and
      report particles.
    why: >-
      Dimensional analysis is a path whose units cancel. Kilograms cannot cancel grams per mole
      until kilograms become grams. Dividing by molar mass leaves moles; multiplying by
      molecules per mole leaves molecules. The unit trail is also an error detector.

  - group: EQUIL
    call: Go to the carbon ledger, in Plant Control
    task: Count the legitimate carbon streams and close the ledger.
    title: Close the first carbon ledger
    at: ledger
    reason: The first repair decision depends on whether carbon is actually missing.
    concept: Balanced equations and stoichiometry as an atom ledger
    takesAsRead: Atoms, molecules and ions, and the mole as a way of counting them
    place: Plant Control
    # KEYSTONE: Follow the amount · COMBINE · L2 · clue
    scene: >-
      Use the corrected counts to total every carbon stream and decide whether enough carbon is
      actually missing to support a large methane leak. The closed balance will determine
      whether leak work should continue.
    motivation: >-
      If carbon is still inside the process, the supposedly missing methane may never have
      leaked.
    background:
    - >-
      Matter is described at several levels. An atom is one neutral element unit; a molecule is
      bonded atoms with no net charge; an ion has a net charge. Chemists convert a measured mass to
      moles using molar mass, then convert moles to particles with Avogadro's number, 6.022 x 10^23
      mol^-1. A balanced equation is an atom ledger: coefficients may change the number of
      molecules, but atoms are not created or destroyed.
    assumes:
    - atoms are neither created nor destroyed by a chemical reaction
    format: BALANCE
    question: Count the legitimate carbon streams and close the ledger.
    relationship: carbon accounted for = carbon as methane + carbon as recycled carbon dioxide + carbon vented
    balance:
      total: { amount: 100.0, unit: kmol C, label: Carbon into the plant as carbon dioxide }
      tolerance: 0.1
      streams:
      - { id: methane, label: Carbon collected as methane, value: 81.6, unit: kmol C }
      - { id: recycle, label: Carbon still in the loop as unreacted carbon dioxide, value: 17.9, unit: kmol C }
      - { id: vented, label: Carbon in sampled and vented gas, value: 0.3, unit: kmol C }
      - { id: water, label: Coproduct water collected, value: 0, countable: false, unit: kmol C,
          unitNote: Water carries hydrogen and oxygen and no carbon at all. }
      - { id: unassigned, label: Carbon in no stream anybody has measured, value: 0.2, hidden: true, unit: kmol C }
      commit: Report the total carbon
    answerText: >-
      The ledger accounts for 99.8 kmol C and leaves only 0.2 kmol C unassigned.
    why: >-
      One mole of CO2 and one mole of CH4 each contain one mole of carbon atoms. Add carbon
      streams as carbon, not total molecular mass. A 0.2% residual can be measurement
      uncertainty; it cannot explain an 18% methane schedule shortfall. This does not yet prove
      there is no leak, but it makes a large carbon leak a poor explanation.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the rooms

# =========================================================== MISSION 2
# Every player-facing line is v10's, verbatim. What is mine is the mapping:
# curriculum area, `at:` fixture, canonical format and payload conversion.
#
# THE ONE DEFECT THE LAST BUILD FLAGGED IS PAID. v10's stop 7 answer text read
