- title: The Feedstock Problem
  card:
    header: 14 SHIFTS UNTIL THE LAUNCH WINDOW CLOSES
    title: THE FEEDSTOCK PROBLEM
    goNow: >-
      Go to the Atmosphere Intake and meet Ingrid Sundqvist, the production and catalyst lead, at
      the compressor log desk.
    body: >-
      The carbon ledger makes a large leak unlikely, but the plant still cannot make enough methane.
      The Sabatier reactor combines carbon dioxide from Martian air with hydrogen recovered from
      water. At the Atmosphere Intake, calculate how much methane each supply can support and
      identify which one runs out first. By the end of the mission, decide whether to push the
      intake harder or investigate hydrogen delivery.
    objective: >-
      Determine which ingredient runs out first and whether the carbon-dioxide intake needs repair.
  objective: >-
    Determine which ingredient runs out first and whether the carbon-dioxide intake needs repair.
  stake: >-
    The carbon ledger makes a large leak unlikely, but the plant still cannot make enough methane.
    The Sabatier reactor combines carbon dioxide from Martian air with hydrogen recovered from
    water. At the Atmosphere Intake, calculate how much methane each supply can support and
    identify which one runs out first. By the end of the mission, decide whether to push the
    intake harder or investigate hydrogen delivery.
  primer:
  - Balance an equation before using its coefficients.
  - Compare every reactant by the amount of the same product it could make; the smaller product amount identifies the limiting reactant.
  - A correct calculation may rule out the easiest explanation without revealing the real fault.
  takeaway: Scarce diagnostic material creates a real trade-off between learning and output.
  segue: >-
    Mission decision: Do not push the air intake. Test the hydrogen line instead. The captured
    carbon dioxide could make 2405 kg of methane, more than this shift needs. Hydrogen runs out
    first. A test pulse now moves toward the Hydrogen Store.

  beats:
  - id: arrival
    on: { enter: true, at: INTAKE }
    world: >-
      Ice coats the intake housing while the compressor shakes the platform. Sundqvist points to the
      full-shift capture log.
    stage:
      flash: true
      wall:
      - text: 'FULL-SHIFT CO2 CAPTURE: LOGGED'
        tone: warn
    bubbles:
    - who: sundqvist
      say: >-
        This machine pulls carbon dioxide from the Martian air. If it cannot collect enough, the
        reactor cannot make the methane that takes us home. Before I push it harder, tell me whether
        the air supply is actually the problem.

  - id: after-workflow
    on: { after: [1] }
    world: The conversion cards lock into one unit-cancelling chain.
    panel: CO2 MASS -> CO2 MOLES -> CH4 MOLES -> CH4 MASS
    panelTone: ok
    bubbles:
    - who: sundqvist
      say: >-
        Use the captured amount. Tell me what this intake could make before I touch the compressor.

  - id: after-yield
    on: { after: [2] }
    world: The proposed compressor-overdrive control becomes unavailable.
    stage:
      wall:
      - text: 'THEORETICAL CH4: 2405 kg'
        tone: ok
      - text: 'REQUIRED CH4: 2000 kg'
        tone: ok
      - text: 'CO2 SUPPLY: SUFFICIENT'
        tone: ok
    panel: 'THEORETICAL CH4: 2405 kg / REQUIRED CH4: 2000 kg / CO2 SUPPLY: SUFFICIENT'
    bubbles:
    - who: sundqvist
      say: >-
        Then the intake is not starving us. We have enough carbon dioxide to meet the launch target.
    - who: abiola
